StellarMasses
2016-11-16
Edward Taylor <entaylor@swin.edu.au>

This DMU provides stellar masses, restframe photometry, and other
ancillary stellar population parameters from stellar population fits
to multiband SEDs for all GAMA DR3 galaxies in the equatorial survey
regions with 0 < z < 0.65 and nQ > 1 from <a href="/dr3/schema/table.php?name=TilingCat">TilingCat</a>. The fits are
constrained to the restframe wavelength range between 3000 and 11000
Angstrom (approximately u through Y). Maximum redshifts for target
selection are also given, for use in 1/Vmax-style incompleteness
corrections.

Two tables are provided by this DMU. The table StellarMasses contains
the results of performing the fits to the photometry of <a href="/dr3/schema/table.php?name=ApMatchedCat">ApMatchedCat</a>,
whereas StellarMassesLambdar contains the results of using the
photometry of <a href="/dr3/schema/table.php?name=LambdarCat">LambdarCat</a> as input.

VERY IMPORTANT NOTE:
The values in these tables are based on aperture (AUTO or LAMBDAR)
photometry, which may miss a significant fraction of a galaxy's
light. Make sure you understand the need for and meaning of the
quantity 'fluxscale', which is described in the section 'BASED ON
MATCHED APERTURE PHOTOMETRY' below.

______________________________________________________________________________

DMU dependencies:
	TilingCat               - heliocentric redshifts, quality flags, etc.
	ApMatchedCat            - matched aperture SED photometry
	LambdarCat  		- matched aperture SED photometry
	SersicCatSDSS           - total r-band Sersic magnitudes
	GalacticExtinction      - foreground dust values
	DistancesFrames         - flow corrected redshifts for distances
______________________________________________________________________________



STELLAR POPULATION SYNTHESIS MODELLING: ______________________________________

The data provided by this table have been derived through stellar
population synthesis modelling of broadband photometry. In the fits,
each photometric point is weighted so that the fits are effectively to
the fixed restframe wavelength range 3000 to 11000 Angstrom for all
galaxies at all redshifts. The modelling is done using the Bruzual &
Charlot (2003) stellar evolution models, assuming a Chabrier (2003)
stellar initial mass function (IMF) and the Calzetti et al. (2000)
dust curve. The SPS models used in the fitting are defined via four
parameters: e-folding time for the (exponentially declining) star
formation history, time since formation (i.e. age), stellar
metallicity, and dust attenuation (Section 3.1 of Taylor et al.
2011). The SPS grid spans the range 0 < z < 0.65; objects with 
z > 0.65 have not been fit. (Talk to Edward Taylor if you need/would
like mass estimates for these galaxies.) The values of all derived
parameters given in the table, including the formal uncertainties,
have been derived in a Bayesian way (Sections 3.2-3.4 of Taylor et
al. 2011).

DISTANCES AND COSMOLOGY: _____________________________________________________

All values are derived for the concordance (Omega_m, Omega_L, h) =
(0.3, 0.7, 0.7) cosmology. Stellar population parameters including
ages, dust extinctions, and mass-to-light ratios are cosmology
independent, inasmuch as they pertain to the stellar populations at
the time of observation. Restframe luminosities and thus the total
stellar mass do depend on distance, and so on h^-2. These can be
scaled in the usual way, such that a higher value of h implies a lower
distance, and thus a lower luminosity/mass (see below). Note that
distances have been computed using the flow-corrected redshifts
'Z_TONRY' given in DistancesFrames. These values have been derived
using the Tonry et al. (2000) flow model for very low redshifts, and
then tapering to a CMB-centric frame for z > 0.03, as described by
Baldry et al. (2012), and are reproduced in this catalogue.

BASED ON MATCHED APERTURE PHOTOMETRY: ________________________________________

The results given in table StellarMasses have been derived from
matched aperture photometry, i.e. the r-defined AUTO photometry from
ApMatchedCat (Hill et al. 2011; Liske et al. 2015; Driver et
al. 2015). In StellarMassesLambdar results are also given for the
photometry from LambdarCat derived using the code LAMBDAR (Wright
et al. 2016). This code attempts to derive consistent and deblended
photometry across all bands. (Note that the convention in both of
these photometry catalogues is to refer to the SDSS z-band and the
VISTA Z-band filters as 'z' and 'X', respectively. This convention is
perpetuated in this DMU). Neither the AUTO nor the LAMBDAR photometry
purports to necessarily represent the total flux in any particular
band.

AN APERTURE CORRECTION IS THEREFORE REQUIRED for integrated quantities
(i.e. stellar mass or luminosity), in order to account for mass/flux
that falls beyond the finite AUTO aperture used for the SEDs. The
column 'fluxscale' is provided for this purpose. The quantity
'fluxscale' is the (linear) ratio between the r-band (AUTO/LAMBDAR)
aperture flux and the total r-band flux inferred from fitting a Sersic
profile (truncated at 10 Re, i.e. 'GAL_MAG_10RE_R', taken from
<a href="/dr3/schema/table.php?name=SersicCatSDSS">SersicCatSDSS</a>).

THIS CORRECTION IS NOT APPLIED BY DEFAULT for two reasons. First, not
all objects have a Sersic-fit magnitude owing to failures in either
the SExtractor detection/segmentation or the GALFIT Sersic fitting
procedures (see Kelvin et al. 2012). These objects can be identified
as those with 'fluxscale' = -9999. Second, particularly for the
fainter galaxies with higher (but unquantified) uncertainties in the
Sersic-fit magnitudes, the flux scaling implied by the Sersic
magnitudes is not necessarily physically sensible. For many galaxies,
the Sersic magnitude is less than the aperture magnitude, which is
logically inconsistent. Further, for some galaxies, the implied flux
scaling leads to masses that are unrealistically high (log M* >> 13).

THE USER IS THEREFORE TRUSTED TO APPLY THESE CORRECTIONS, but with the
understanding that some care is required. The recommended mass to use
is thus:
   log10 M*,total = logmstar + log10(fluxscale) - 2 log10(h/0.7), 
and the recommended absolute magnitude in the X-band is:
   M_X,total = absmag_X - 2.5 log10(fluxscale) + 5 log10(h/0.7).

Users should check to ensure that this makes sense (e.g. check that
'fluxscale' is greater than or equal to unity, if only approximately,
and that the magnitude of the correction is not a factor of many) for
their specific object(s) of interest. Note, too, that this correction
introduces an additional source of uncertainty in the total
luminosities and masses that is not quantified in these tables.

GOODNESS OF FIT STATISTIC: ___________________________________________________

StellarMasses includes the Posterior Predictive P-value (PPP) as a
goodness of fit statistic, which can be roughly thought of as a
Bayesian analogue to the frequentist reduced chi-squared. The PPP is a
useful tool for checking whether or to what extent the model is
consistent with the data. Given the observed data and the posterior
PDF on the properties of the model, the PPP quantifies the fraction of
future observations that would be predicted to be as discrepant as the
observed data. As an example, a PPP < 0.01 could be taken to imply
that the current data invalidate even the 'most likely' model at 99%
confidence.

Whether and how best to use this information is probably application
specific, and is left to the user to decide. In general terms, a PPP~0
generally indicates a likely catastrophic failure of the SED fit, but
can also indicate, for example, just one bad photometric point.
Individual SED fits with PPP < 0.2 should be treated with some
suspicion. A PPP of ~0.5 implies a perfect fit. A PPP of ~1 implies an
improbably good fit - either the observational uncertainties have been
overestimated, or the data are being overfit.

DIFFERENT MASS ESTIMATORS: ___________________________________________________

There are three different masses given in the catalogues:

  * logmstar -- this is the total mass of all luminous material, at
      the time of observation.
  * logmremnants -- this is the total mass of all non- or sub-luminous
      stellar remnants (e.g. white dwarfs), at the time of observation.
  * logmintsfh -- this is the total mass of all stars formed, obtained
      by integrating over the implied SFH, up to the time of
      observation.

Note that the difference between the combined mass of stars and
remnants on the one hand, and the integral of the SFH on the other, is
mass lost in stellar winds or other ejecta. In this sense, embedded in
the values of logmintsfh is the assumption that none of this 'lost'
mass is 'recycled' back into new generations of stars.

It is envisaged that most users will want to use the 'logmstar's,
which are the most directly comparable to what is most often used in
the literature. Some recent studies also include stellar remnants in
their stellar masses, but this is not currently - or not yet - the
norm. Finally, the integral of the implied SFH gives the total mass of
all stars formed, from the earliest times up to the epoch of
observation. In most cases, 'logmintsfh' is the most directly
comparable to the stellar masses that are output by simulations.

RESTFRAME PHOTOMETRY AND COLOURS: ____________________________________________

All of the photometry provided by this DMU has been inferred from
the SPS fits as an attribute of the SP models. All restframe
photometry uses AB zeropoints and is k-corrected to redshift 0, with
no evolution corrections. The table contains restframe (g - i) and 
(u - r) colours (in the columns 'gminusi' and 'uminusi', respectively)
as well as restframe photometry in individual bands (e.g. 'absmag_g'
and 'absmag_i'). Because in the fitting process, ugriz fluxes are
highly correlated with one another, the formal uncertainties on
'gminusi' or 'uminusr' cannot in general be derived from the
'delrfphot'. It is thus preferable to use, e.g., 'gminusi' and the
associated uncertainty 'delgminusi' rather than 'absmag_g' -
'absmag_i'.

Further, note that the fact that the fits are constrained to the
restframe wavelength range 3000-11000 Angstrom means that the
restframe photometry in the J and K bands are extrapolations of the
fit-to data.

INTRINSIC VERSUS EFFECTIVE RESTFRAME PHOTOMETRY: _____________________________

The quantities 'absmag_X' should be understood as *effective*
restframe absolute magnitudes in the X-band; that is, the absolute
magnitudes corresponding to the light the observer actually sees from
the galaxy, without correction for extinction within the galaxy
itself. In contrast, the quantities 'absmag_X_stars' give *intrinsic*
absolute magnitudes in the X-band, which should be understood to be
the total light emitted by stars; that is, corrected for internal dust
extinction according to 'extBV'. In both cases, these values are
derived from the SPS fits as properties of the stellar populations in
a similar way to ages, metallicities, mass-to-light ratios,
etc. (i.e. a la Equation 5 of Taylor et al. 2011).

MAXIMAL DETECTION/SELECTION REDSHIFTS FOR INCOMPLETENESS CORRECTIONS: ________

In order to facilitate the derivation of completeness-corrected number
densities, limiting redshifts have been derived for four r-band
selection limits: r < 17.8 (i.e. SDSS main sample target selection); r
< 19.0 (DR2 and DR3 limits in G09 and G12); r < 19.4 (G09 and G15
SURVEY_CLASS >= 6 target selection for GAMA I and DR2 limit in G15);
and r < 19.8 (G12 SURVEY_CLASS >= 6 target selection for GAMA I, as
well as the final SURVEY_CLASS >= 4 limit for GAMA II and for DR3 in
G15). All three of these selections are phrased in terms of r-band
Petrosian magnitudes, as given in the SDSS DR7 catalogues, AFTER
correction for foreground (Galactic) extinction, and quoted as
'R_PETRO' in TilingCat. The limiting redshifts are the maximum
redshift at which any given galaxy would satisfy the r-band selection
in question. These values have been derived in the course of the SPS
fits, using the single best fitting template to estimate how the
r-band magnitude varies with redshift/distance. Note that these
limiting redshifts should be understood as being flow-corrected, not
heliocentric (see Section 3.5 of Taylor et al. 2011). Users should
bear this in mind when selecting or binning galaxies by redshift.

As an example for how to use these values for GAMA I, imagining that
the user is considering galaxies with z < z_limit, the effective
survey volume for each individual object can be calculated as:

  V_max ~ 96 * comovingVolume(min(zmax_19p4, zlimit)) 
	+ 48 * comovingVolume(min(zmax_19p8, zlimit)).

For GAMA II, the expression is rather simpler:

  V_max ~ 180 * comovingVolume(min(zmax_19p8, zlimit)).

Talk to, for example, Edward Taylor, Ivan Baldry, or Peder Norberg if
you are worried about how to do this.

SUMMARY PLOTS: _______________________________________________________________

This DMU also provides a summary plot for each object (column
URL_PNG), which shows the object's full SED as well as the fit. This
plot is part of the information displayed by the <a href="/dr3/tools/sov.php">Single Object Viewer</a>.

KNOWN ISSUES: ________________________________________________________________

* There are a number of very low redshift objects that are given
  unphysically low values for 'logmstar', e.g. 'logmstar' << 6. These
  are almost all stars, and can largely be excluded by considering 
  z > 0.003.

* There are a very few objects for which the fitting procedure has
  failed. For these objects, all SP parameters are given as -99,
  i.e. as missing data.

* There are a number of objects that are given one or more of the
  'zmax' >= 0.65. This corresponds to the maximum redshift covered by
  the SPL grid. This is true for many of the galaxies where the fits
  have failed (see above), but also for galaxies with reasonable SP
  parameters. For these "okay" galaxies, this should be interpreted as
  a lower limit to the values of the 'zmax'. In other words, the
  implication is that GAMA is volume limited for such objects for 
  z <= 0.65.

* There are a number of objects with Z ~ 0.05, which corresponds to
  the maximum metallicity contained in the SPS grids. The mass
  estimates for these objects are unlikely to be significantly
  compromised, but the age and dust extinction values may be biased.

